  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Former Career
    
    Kaela is a Nightshelf merchant, formerly a master ice-carver whose work decorated the forges of Thrall☽ Vavmastius, a thermal vent deep in the Nightshelf tunnels. By contemporary accounts she could read ice with exceptional precision — sensing where fissures would run before the first pick struck — and her name carried weight in the deeper passages.
    
    That career ended at the Bastion Apex, an ancient crystalline structure in the deep ice. Kaela was present for a forbidden blood-rite performed there — the same ritual documented in chronicle accounts of the Apex's desecration, conducted by practitioners in ash-gray robes who opened a dark fissure and awakened something beneath the ice. The rite left Kaela's flippers trembling permanently and the ice silent to her touch. She reportedly descended to the corrupted chamber afterward and spoke an apology to the Apex itself. Whether this is literal testimony or chronicle embellishment, the practical consequence is unambiguous: she lost her ability to read ice and never recovered it.
    
    ## Present Life
    
    Kaela now operates as a merchant in the upper tunnels. She makes twice-monthly fire-tea deliveries to the reclusive Magister ☽'Naevaeum~, a hermit dwelling on a ledge near Thrall☽ Vavmastius. She leaves the tea without comment or inquiry. When ☽'Naevaeum~ began sharing the ledge with Zhachelang zhihi'Opalum, an Aurora Stack coordinator, Kaela set out a third cup without remark. At another stop — likely the Smelt shrine — she sets two cups each morning. Someone drinks the second. She does not say who.
    
    She checks lamp-oil for younger miners in the upper passages and deflects questions about why she no longer goes deep.
    
    ## What She Carries
    
    Kaela keeps the ice-pick of Tormin, a foreman's apprentice who died in the corrupted tunnels of Sleek☽ Caemomot, hung unused and unsharpened in her dwelling. She carries a token belonging to Korvik, a Nightshelf penguin who pulled drowning penguins from the meltwater during the Tide Fall Blasphemy and did not survive.
    
    She operated an aurora-silk exchange on the Forum Floe for seventeen years. After the Floe's collapse, she was found three days later sitting in her ruined stall, arranging fabric samples on a counter sloping toward a crevasse. Chronicle accounts attribute this to old memories released from the cracking ice — something witnessed that left her unable to register the destruction around her. She never spoke of what she saw.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
